	abstract = {Whip targeting (to hit) is a challenging motor control task owing to movement rapidness ({\textless} 1.5{\textbackslash} s) and accuracy, as well as tool flexibility. A learning-based arm control framework is proposed for manipulating a whip to hit targets in this paper. The framework combines a joint motion planner learned by the algorithm soft actor-critic ({SAC}) with online impedance adaptation control ({OIAC}). It was validated on a simulated arm and whip system with 54 degrees-of-freedom ({DOFs}), whose objective is to minimize the distance between the whip tip and a target in a 3D space. As a result, our integration of data-driven and physics-based algorithms allows for 89\% improvement of hitting effectiveness and {\textgreater}72\% reductions of joint motion tracking control errors, compared to traditional learning and control algorithms. This integration provides a novel proof-of-concept solution to fast targeting control in flexible object manipulation11https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y6qvPOlTjOk.},
