	abstract = {This paper presents a learning-based control framework for fast ({\textless} 1.5 s) and accurate manipulation of a flexible object, i.e., whip targeting. The framework consists of a motion planner learned or optimized by an algorithm, Online Impedance Adaptation Control ({OIAC}), a sim2real mechanism, and a visual feedback component. The experimental results show that a soft actor-critic algorithm outperforms three Deep Reinforcement Learning ({DRL}), a nonlinear optimization, and a genetic algorithm in learning generalization of motion planning. It can greatly reduce average learning trials (to {\textless} 20\$\${\textbackslash}\%\$\$of others) and maximize average rewards (to {\textgreater} 3 times of others). Besides, motion tracking errors are greatly reduced to 13.29\$\${\textbackslash}\%\$\$and 22.36\$\${\textbackslash}\%\$\$of constant impedance control by the {OIAC} of the proposed framework. In addition, the trajectory similarity between simulated and physical whips is 89.09\$\${\textbackslash}\%\$\$. The presented framework provides a new method integrating data-driven and physics-based algorithms for controlling fast and accurate arm manipulation of a flexible object.},
